    "lem-pc-skeleton-separation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-d-separation-in-a-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite directed acyclic graph** (DAG) is a finite vertex set $V$ with an\narrow set $E\\subseteq V\\times V$ and no directed cycle. A **path** from $u$ to\n$v$ is a finite sequence of distinct vertices beginning at $u$ and ending at\n$v$ in which each consecutive pair is joined by an arrow in either direction;\nthe arrows are not required to point along the sequence. An internal vertex of\na path is a **collider on that path** when the two arrows on the path both point\ninto it, and is a **noncollider** otherwise. A vertex $w$ is a **descendant** of\n$v$ if there is a directed path from $v$ to $w$; for this definition\n$\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ includes $v$ itself as well as its descendants.\n\nFor pairwise disjoint vertex sets $X,Y,C\\subseteq V$, a path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is **active given $C$** when every internal noncollider is outside $C$ and\nevery internal collider has at least one member of $\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ in\n$C$. The set $C$ **d-separates** $X$ and $Y$ in $G$ when no path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is active given $C$; write $X\\perp_d Y\\mid C$ in $G$. The empty set of\nvertices is allowed for any of $X,Y,C$; in particular d-separation is vacuous\nif either endpoint set is empty.\n\nFor $T\\subseteq V$, $G_{\\underline T}$ is obtained by deleting every arrow\nwhose tail lies in $T$, and $G_{\\overline T}$ by deleting every arrow whose\nhead lies in $T$. These are graph operations only; they do not by themselves\nspecify a probability law under intervention.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $i$ and $j$ are adjacent, the one-edge path between them has no internal vertices, so it is active given every $S\\subseteq V\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$ by [F1]. Hence no such $S$ d-separates them.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Suppose $i$ and $j$ are nonadjacent. Acyclicity implies that at least one of $i,j$ is not a descendant of the other; after interchanging labels, assume $j$ is not a descendant of $i$. Since $i,j$ are nonadjacent, $j$ is a non-descendant non-parent of $i$. Put $S=\\operatorname{pa}_G(i)$. Then $S$ excludes both endpoints. Consider any path $i=v_0,v_1,\\ldots,v_r=j$. If its first arrow points into $i$, then $v_1\\in S$ and the occurrence of $v_1$ is a noncollider, because the arrow $v_1\\to i$ has its tail at $v_1$; the path is blocked. If the first arrow points out of $i$, follow arrows that point forward along the path until the first arrow that points backward. Such a reversal must occur because $j$ is not a descendant of $i$. At the reversal vertex $v_t$, both incident arrows point into $v_t$, so it is a collider and the initial segment is a directed path $i\\to\\cdots\\to v_t$. If $v_t$ had a descendant $p\\in S$, then $i\\to\\cdots\\to v_t\\to\\cdots\\to p\\to i$ would be a directed cycle. Thus this collider has no descendant in $S$, and the path is blocked by [F1]. Every path is blocked, so $S$ d-separates $i$ and $j$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The adjacent and nonadjacent cases prove both implications.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "reason": "The constructed parent set in step 1.2 may be empty. An inward first edge would require a parent and therefore cannot occur in that case; an outward first edge still encounters a collider with no descendant in the empty set, so every path is blocked.",
          "evidence": "The constructed parent set in step 1.2 may be empty. An inward first edge would require a parent and therefore cannot occur in that case; an outward first edge still encounters a collider with no descendant in the empty set, so every path is blocked.",
