    "lem-pc-separating-set-collider": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-d-separation-in-a-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite directed acyclic graph** (DAG) is a finite vertex set $V$ with an\narrow set $E\\subseteq V\\times V$ and no directed cycle. A **path** from $u$ to\n$v$ is a finite sequence of distinct vertices beginning at $u$ and ending at\n$v$ in which each consecutive pair is joined by an arrow in either direction;\nthe arrows are not required to point along the sequence. An internal vertex of\na path is a **collider on that path** when the two arrows on the path both point\ninto it, and is a **noncollider** otherwise. A vertex $w$ is a **descendant** of\n$v$ if there is a directed path from $v$ to $w$; for this definition\n$\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ includes $v$ itself as well as its descendants.\n\nFor pairwise disjoint vertex sets $X,Y,C\\subseteq V$, a path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is **active given $C$** when every internal noncollider is outside $C$ and\nevery internal collider has at least one member of $\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ in\n$C$. The set $C$ **d-separates** $X$ and $Y$ in $G$ when no path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is active given $C$; write $X\\perp_d Y\\mid C$ in $G$. The empty set of\nvertices is allowed for any of $X,Y,C$; in particular d-separation is vacuous\nif either endpoint set is empty.\n\nFor $T\\subseteq V$, $G_{\\underline T}$ is obtained by deleting every arrow\nwhose tail lies in $T$, and $G_{\\overline T}$ by deleting every arrow whose\nhead lies in $T$. These are graph operations only; they do not by themselves\nspecify a probability law under intervention.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cpdag-markov-equivalence-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite vertex set $V$. For a DAG $G$ on $V$, its **skeleton** is the\nundirected graph with edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $G$ contains $i\\to j$ or\n$j\\to i$. An **unshielded triple** is a triple of distinct vertices $i,k,j$\nwith edges $i-k$ and $k-j$ in the skeleton and no edge between $i$ and $j$.\nIt is an **unshielded collider** (also called a v-structure) when its arrows are\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$. Two DAGs on $V$ are **Markov equivalent** when they\ninduce the same d-separation relations: for every pairwise disjoint\n$A,B,S\\subseteq V$, $A$ and $B$ are d-separated by $S$ in one DAG exactly\nwhen they are d-separated by $S$ in the other. The equivalence class of $G$ is\ndenoted $[G]$.\n\nA **partially directed acyclic graph** (PDAG) on $V$ is a simple mixed graph\nwhose directed edges contain no directed cycle. A **consistent DAG extension**\nof a PDAG preserves every directed edge, orients every undirected edge, is\nacyclic, and creates no unshielded collider absent from the PDAG. For an\nequivalence class with a common skeleton, its **completed pattern** directs an\nedge exactly when that orientation occurs in every DAG of the class and leaves\nthe edge undirected when both orientations occur. A **completed partially\ndirected acyclic graph** (CPDAG) for $[G]$ is this unique completed pattern\nwhen its consistent DAG extensions are exactly $[G]$. Thus its directed edges\nare compelled, while each undirected edge occurs in both directions among its\nconsistent extensions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $i\\to k\\leftarrow j$, then $k$ is a collider on the length-two path from $i$ to $j$. If $k\\in S$, it is its own conditioned descendant, so this path is active by [F1], contradicting that $S$ separates the endpoints. Therefore $k\\notin S$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "If the unshielded triple is not a v-structure, then $k$ is a noncollider on its length-two path. If $k\\notin S$, that path has no conditioned internal noncollider and no collider, so it is active by [F1], again contradicting separation. Hence a noncollider requires $k\\in S$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The two implications prove the equivalence.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
