    "lem-covered-edge-reversal-preserves-d-separation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-d-separation-ancestral-moral-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For disjoint $X,Y,C\\subseteq V$, $C$ d-separates $X$ and $Y$ in $G$ if and\nonly if deleting $C$ from $\\operatorname{mor}_G(X\\cup Y\\cup C)$ leaves no\nundirected path from $X$ to $Y$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ancestral-moral-graph-of-a-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $S\\subseteq V$, its **ancestral set** is\n$$\\operatorname{An}_G(S):=\\{v\\in V:\\text{there is a directed path from }v\\text{ to some }s\\in S\\},$$\nwhere paths of length zero are allowed, so $S\\subseteq\\operatorname{An}_G(S)$.\nThe induced directed subgraph on this set keeps exactly the arrows of $G$\nwhose two endpoints lie in $\\operatorname{An}_G(S)$. Its **moral graph** is\nthe undirected simple graph obtained by replacing every arrow by an\nundirected edge and joining every distinct pair of parents of a common child\nby an undirected edge; loops and duplicate edges are discarded. This\nundirected graph is the **ancestral moral graph** on $S$ and is denoted\n$\\operatorname{mor}_G(S)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $P=\\operatorname{pa}_G(x)$, so $\\operatorname{pa}_G(y)=P\\cup\\{x\\}$. If reversing the arrow created a directed cycle, that cycle would use the new arrow $y\\to x$, and the remaining part would be a directed path $x\\to\\cdots\\to z\\to y$ in $G$ with $z\\ne x$. Then $z\\in P$, so $z\\to x$ is also in $G$, making the directed cycle $x\\to\\cdots\\to z\\to x$ in $G$, a contradiction. Thus $G'$ is acyclic.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "choose",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "The full moral graphs of $G$ and $G'$ coincide. Their skeletons coincide. The only changed parent sets are $\\operatorname{pa}_G(x)=P$, $\\operatorname{pa}_G(y)=P\\cup\\{x\\}$ and $\\operatorname{pa}_{G'}(x)=P\\cup\\{y\\}$, $\\operatorname{pa}_{G'}(y)=P$. Any pair newly married at $y$ in $G$ or at $x$ in $G'$ already belongs to the clique on $P\\cup\\{x,y\\}$: pairs involving $x$ or $y$ are skeleton edges, and pairs in $P$ are married at both $x$ and $y$. All other parent sets are unchanged, so the moral edge sets are identical.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "Fix pairwise disjoint query sets $A,B,S$ and put $T=A\\cup B\\cup S$. The ancestor sets of $T$ in $G$ and $G'$ differ, if at all, only by $x$ or $y$. Indeed, a directed path whose existence changes must use the reversed edge. A path in $G$ that uses $x\\to y$ and starts at $v\\ne x$ enters $x$ from some $p\\in P$; in $G'$ replace $p\\to x\\to y$ by the unchanged arrow $p\\to y$. Therefore only $x$ can cease to be an ancestor. Symmetrically, a path in $G'$ using $y\\to x$ and starting at $v\\ne y$ enters $y$ from $p\\in P=\\operatorname{pa}_{G'}(y)$; in $G$ replace $p\\to y\\to x$ by $p\\to x$. Therefore only $y$ can become an ancestor. If $x$ is lost, $y$ belongs to both ancestor sets; if $y$ is gained, $x$ belongs to both.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If the ancestor sets are equal, step 1.2 shows the induced ancestral moral graphs are equal. Suppose instead that $x$ is an ancestor only in $G$. Then $x\\notin T$ and $y$ is a common ancestor of $T$. In the moral graph induced on $\\operatorname{An}_G(T)$, every neighbor of $x$ is in $P\\cup\\{y\\}$: its parents lie in $P$; among its children only $y$ can be ancestral, since any other child has an unchanged arrow from $x$ and would make $x$ an ancestor in $G'$; and a marriage through a child other than $y$ would have the same consequence, while a marriage through $y$ uses another parent in $P$. The vertices in $P\\cup\\{y\\}$ form a clique because they are parents of $y$ in $G$. Hence $x$ is simplicial in the ancestral moral graph. The case that $y$ is gained only in $G'$ is symmetric, with its neighbors contained in the clique $P\\cup\\{x\\}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Removing an unconditioned simplicial vertex does not change separation between $A$ and $B$ after deleting $S$: the vertex is not in $T$, and any path through it can be shortened by replacing its two incident edges with the edge joining its neighbors, which exists because the neighborhood is a clique. Conversely, every path in the smaller graph remains in the larger graph. Thus the two ancestral moral graphs separate $A$ and $B$ by $S$ in exactly the same cases. By [F1], $G$ and $G'$ have the same d-separation relations.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.3 defines T=A union B union S; T may be empty."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "reason": "The common parent set P may be empty. Then the cliques P union {y} and P union {x} in step 2.1 are singleton neighborhoods, and the simplicial-vertex path-shortening comparison in step 3.1 still applies without a nonempty parent requirement.",
          "evidence": "The common parent set P may be empty. Then the cliques P union {y} and P union {x} in step 2.1 are singleton neighborhoods, and the simplicial-vertex path-shortening comparison in step 3.1 still applies without a nonempty parent requirement.",
          "template_review": {
