    "thm-dag-markov-equivalence-skeleton-vstructures": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cpdag-markov-equivalence-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite vertex set $V$. For a DAG $G$ on $V$, its **skeleton** is the\nundirected graph with edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $G$ contains $i\\to j$ or\n$j\\to i$. An **unshielded triple** is a triple of distinct vertices $i,k,j$\nwith edges $i-k$ and $k-j$ in the skeleton and no edge between $i$ and $j$.\nIt is an **unshielded collider** (also called a v-structure) when its arrows are\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$. Two DAGs on $V$ are **Markov equivalent** when they\ninduce the same d-separation relations: for every pairwise disjoint\n$A,B,S\\subseteq V$, $A$ and $B$ are d-separated by $S$ in one DAG exactly\nwhen they are d-separated by $S$ in the other. The equivalence class of $G$ is\ndenoted $[G]$.\n\nA **partially directed acyclic graph** (PDAG) on $V$ is a simple mixed graph\nwhose directed edges contain no directed cycle. A **consistent DAG extension**\nof a PDAG preserves every directed edge, orients every undirected edge, is\nacyclic, and creates no unshielded collider absent from the PDAG. For an\nequivalence class with a common skeleton, its **completed pattern** directs an\nedge exactly when that orientation occurs in every DAG of the class and leaves\nthe edge undirected when both orientations occur. A **completed partially\ndirected acyclic graph** (CPDAG) for $[G]$ is this unique completed pattern\nwhen its consistent DAG extensions are exactly $[G]$. Thus its directed edges\nare compelled, while each undirected edge occurs in both directions among its\nconsistent extensions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-pc-skeleton-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG on $V$. For distinct vertices $i,j\\in V$, the pair\n$\\{i,j\\}$ is an edge of the skeleton of $G$ if and only if there is no\n$S\\subseteq V\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$ that d-separates $i$ and $j$ in $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-covered-edge-reversal-preserves-d-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG and let $x\\to y$ be a covered arrow, meaning\n$\\operatorname{pa}_G(y)=\\operatorname{pa}_G(x)\\cup\\{x\\}$. Replacing $x\\to y$\nby $y\\to x$ gives a DAG $G'$ with exactly the same d-separation relations as\n$G$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "First assume that $G_1,G_2$ have the same skeleton and the same unshielded colliders. A finite DAG has a topological order: if every remaining vertex had an incoming arrow, following incoming arrows in the finite graph would repeat a vertex and create a directed cycle; remove a source and repeat. Fix such an order $\\prec$ for $G_1$. If the DAGs differ, choose the $\\prec$-earliest vertex $\\beta$ with $\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\beta)\\setminus\\operatorname{pa}_{G_2}(\\beta)\\ne\\varnothing$, and choose the $\\prec$-latest $\\alpha$ in that difference. Then $\\alpha\\to\\beta$ occurs in $G_1$ and $\\beta\\to\\alpha$ in $G_2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Conversely, assume $G_1,G_2$ are Markov equivalent. If their skeletons differed, [F2] would give a pair separated in one graph but not the other. Thus their skeletons agree. If an unshielded collider $i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ occurred in $G_1$ but not in $G_2$, then $i,j$ are nonadjacent in both. By [F2] choose $S$ that d-separates $i,j$ in $G_1$. The path $i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ shows that no descendant of $k$, including $k$, can lie in $S$, because otherwise that path would be active. In $G_2$, the same unshielded triple has a noncollider at $k$; since $k\\notin S$, the two-edge path is active, so $S$ does not d-separate $i,j$ there. This contradicts Markov equivalence. Interchanging $G_1,G_2$ rules out a collider present only in $G_2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\gamma\\ne\\alpha$ be another parent of $\\beta$ in $G_1$. If $\\gamma$ were not a parent of $\\alpha$, then either $\\gamma$ and $\\alpha$ would be nonadjacent, making $\\alpha\\to\\beta\\leftarrow\\gamma$ an unshielded collider absent from $G_2$, or they would be adjacent with $\\alpha\\to\\gamma$ in $G_1$. The first case contradicts equality of colliders; in the second case, $G_2$ cannot retain both $\\alpha\\to\\gamma$ and $\\gamma\\to\\beta$, because together with $\\beta\\to\\alpha$ they form a directed cycle. If $\\alpha\\to\\gamma$ is reversed in $G_2$, then $\\gamma\\prec\\beta$ is an earlier vertex with a differing parent; if $\\gamma\\to\\beta$ is reversed, then $\\gamma$ is a later member than $\\alpha$ of $\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\beta)\\setminus\\operatorname{pa}_{G_2}(\\beta)$. Both contradict the choices in step 1.1. Hence every parent of $\\beta$ other than $\\alpha$ is a parent of $\\alpha$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "To prove $\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\alpha)\\subseteq\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\beta)$, suppose instead that $\\gamma\\to\\alpha$ in $G_1$ but $\\gamma\\notin\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\beta)$. Minimality of $\\beta$ implies $\\gamma\\to\\alpha$ is also present in $G_2$; otherwise the earlier vertex $\\alpha$ would already have a differing parent. If $\\gamma$ were not adjacent to $\\beta$, then $G_2$ would contain the unshielded collider $\\beta\\to\\alpha\\leftarrow\\gamma$, while $G_1$ has the noncollider chain $\\gamma\\to\\alpha\\to\\beta$, contradicting equality of colliders. Thus $\\gamma$ is adjacent to $\\beta$. In $G_1$ it must be oriented $\\gamma\\to\\beta$, since $\\beta\\to\\gamma$ would make a directed cycle with $\\gamma\\to\\alpha\\to\\beta$. This contradicts the assumption that $\\gamma$ is not a parent of $\\beta$. Therefore the inclusion holds. Together with step 2.1, $\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\beta)=\\operatorname{pa}_{G_1}(\\alpha)\\cup\\{\\alpha\\}$, so $\\alpha\\to\\beta$ is covered.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Reverse this covered arrow in $G_1$. By [F3] the result remains a DAG and is Markov equivalent to $G_1$. It has the same skeleton, and it has the same unshielded colliders: an edge in an unshielded collider cannot be covered, because the other parent of the collider would then also be a parent of the edge's tail and hence adjacent to it. Exactly one edge now agrees with $G_2$, so the finite number of edge-orientation disagreements decreases by one. If any disagreement remains, choose the earliest differing parent set again; the argument in steps 1.1–3.1 applies because the skeleton and collider set are unchanged. Thus finitely many covered reversals transform $G_1$ into $G_2$, and each reversal preserves d-separation.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-5.1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The two directions prove the equivalence.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
