    "thm-gaussian-zero-partial-correlation-is-conditional-independence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nondegenerate-multivariate-normal-law-has-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $d\\ge1$, $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\Sigma$ be a real symmetric positive-definite $d\\times d$ matrix. If $X\\sim N_d(m,\\Sigma)$, then its law has Lebesgue density\n$$f_{m,\\Sigma}(x)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\det(\\Sigma)^{-1/2} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-m)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(x-m)\\right), \\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the axiom of choice so that the library's conditional-expectation\nclasses are available. Let $Y$ and $Z$ be random elements and let\n$\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ be a sigma-algebra. We say that $Y$ and $Z$ are\n**conditionally independent given $\\mathcal G$**, and write\n$Y\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Z\\mid\\mathcal G$, if for every pair of bounded measurable\nreal functions $f,g$,\n$$ \\mathbb E[f(Y)g(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G] =\\mathbb E[f(Y)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\, \\mathbb E[g(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\quad\\text{a.s.} $$\nThis is an equality of almost-everywhere classes and hence does not depend on\nrepresentatives.\n\nFor sigma-algebras $\\mathcal H_1,\\mathcal H_2\\subseteq\\mathcal F$, the notation\n$\\mathcal H_1\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\mathcal H_2\\mid\\mathcal G$ means the same identity\nfor every bounded $\\mathcal H_1$-measurable $U$ and bounded\n$\\mathcal H_2$-measurable $V$.\n\nThe definition is symmetric. If $\\mathcal G=\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$ modulo\nnull sets, it reduces to ordinary independence. If one side is\n$\\mathcal G$-measurable, conditional independence is automatic because that\nfactor is already known when conditioning. The choices of the zero function or\nthe constant-one function cause no exceptional case.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\n[[def-measurable-rectangle]].\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-independent-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be random elements on a\ncommon probability space. For each $i$, write\n\n$$\\sigma(X_i):=\\{X_i^{-1}(B):B\\in\\Sigma_i\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal F.$$\n\nThe family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is **independent** when the sigma-algebras\n$(\\sigma(X_i))_{i\\in I}$ are independent in the sense of\n[[def-independent-sigma-algebras-and-events]].\n\nWhen $\\Sigma_i$ is a Borel sigma-algebra, this agrees with the notation\n$\\sigma(X_i)$ from [[def-sigma-algebra-generated-by-a-function]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Partition $X=(Y,Z)$ with $Y=(X_i,X_j)$ and $Z=X_S$; if $S=\\varnothing$, omit the $Z$ block. For nonempty $S$, $\\Sigma_{S,S}$ is positive definite because every nonzero vector supported on $S$ has positive $\\Sigma$-quadratic form, so it is invertible. Put $A=\\Sigma_{Y,S}\\Sigma_{S,S}^{-1}$ and $U=Y-\\mu_Y-A(Z-\\mu_Z)$. Then $\\operatorname{Cov}(U,Z)=0$ and $\\operatorname{Cov}(U)=K_{ij\\mid S}$. For every nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^2$, the quadratic form of $\\Sigma$ at $(u,-\\Sigma_{S,S}^{-1}\\Sigma_{S,Y}u)$ equals $u^TK_{ij\\mid S}u>0$. Thus $K_{ij\\mid S}$ is positive definite; for empty $S$ this follows directly from the principal $2\\times2$ covariance block.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The vector $(U,Z)$ is a linear image of $X$, hence is jointly Gaussian by [F1]. Its covariance is block diagonal with blocks $K_{ij\\mid S}$ and $\\Sigma_{S,S}$. By [F2], the joint density factors as the product of the two Gaussian densities, since the inverse and determinant of a block-diagonal covariance split by blocks. Integrating over measurable rectangles shows $U$ and $Z$ are independent by [F5]. It follows that, conditionally on $Z$, $Y$ is the translate $\\mu_Y+A(Z-\\mu_Z)+U$ and has conditional covariance $K_{ij\\mid S}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "If $(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12}=0$, the positive-definite bivariate Gaussian density of $U$ factors into its one-coordinate densities. Thus the coordinates of $U$ are independent. Since $U$ is independent of $Z$, the conditional law of $X_i,X_j$ given $Z$ is the product of its conditional marginals. Integrating bounded measurable functions against this product conditional law gives the identity in [F3], hence conditional independence.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.2",
          "step": "3.2",
          "claim": "Conversely assume the conditional independence in the Statement. Apply [F3] to the sine and cosine functions of each coordinate and combine the four real identities to obtain factorization of the conditional characteristic function at $(1,1)$. By step 2.1, the joint conditional characteristic function is the product of the marginal factors times $\\exp(-(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12})$. All other factors are nonzero; therefore $\\exp(-(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12})=1$. The covariance entry is real, so $(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12}=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The diagonal entries of $K_{ij\\mid S}$ are positive by step 1.1. Its off-diagonal entry is zero exactly when the normalized entry $\\rho_{ij\\mid S}$ is zero, proving both implications.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
