    "lem-meek-rules-complete-for-cpdag": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cpdag-markov-equivalence-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite vertex set $V$. For a DAG $G$ on $V$, its **skeleton** is the\nundirected graph with edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $G$ contains $i\\to j$ or\n$j\\to i$. An **unshielded triple** is a triple of distinct vertices $i,k,j$\nwith edges $i-k$ and $k-j$ in the skeleton and no edge between $i$ and $j$.\nIt is an **unshielded collider** (also called a v-structure) when its arrows are\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$. Two DAGs on $V$ are **Markov equivalent** when they\ninduce the same d-separation relations: for every pairwise disjoint\n$A,B,S\\subseteq V$, $A$ and $B$ are d-separated by $S$ in one DAG exactly\nwhen they are d-separated by $S$ in the other. The equivalence class of $G$ is\ndenoted $[G]$.\n\nA **partially directed acyclic graph** (PDAG) on $V$ is a simple mixed graph\nwhose directed edges contain no directed cycle. A **consistent DAG extension**\nof a PDAG preserves every directed edge, orients every undirected edge, is\nacyclic, and creates no unshielded collider absent from the PDAG. For an\nequivalence class with a common skeleton, its **completed pattern** directs an\nedge exactly when that orientation occurs in every DAG of the class and leaves\nthe edge undirected when both orientations occur. A **completed partially\ndirected acyclic graph** (CPDAG) for $[G]$ is this unique completed pattern\nwhen its consistent DAG extensions are exactly $[G]$. Thus its directed edges\nare compelled, while each undirected edge occurs in both directions among its\nconsistent extensions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.3",
            "5.1",
            "7.1",
            "8.1",
            "9.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-pc-causal-discovery",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** A finite vertex set $V$, a perfect conditional-independence oracle\nfor a law in the causally sufficient discovery regime, and a fixed total order\nof $V$. For every adjacent pair and conditioning set $S$ tested below, the\noracle returns whether $X_i$ and $X_j$ are conditionally independent given\n$X_S$.\n\n**Output:** An undirected skeleton $C$, a separator record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$\nfor each deleted edge, and a partially directed graph $P$.\n\n1. Initialize $C$ to the complete undirected graph on $V$ and set the level\n$\\ell=0$.\n\n2. In the fixed lexicographic order of unordered vertex pairs, process each\npair $i-j$ that is still an edge of $C$. First test every $\\ell$-element\nsubset of $\\operatorname{Adj}_C(i)\\setminus\\{j\\}$ in lexicographic order;\nthen test every $\\ell$-element subset of\n$\\operatorname{Adj}_C(j)\\setminus\\{i\\}$ in lexicographic order. On the first\noracle result of conditional independence given a tested set $S$, delete\n$i-j$, record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)=\\operatorname{Sep}(j,i)=S$, and do\nnot test that pair again. If no set separates the pair, leave it adjacent.\nAdjacency sets are read from the current graph, including deletions already\nmade at level $\\ell$.\n\n3. If at least one remaining edge has an endpoint with at least $\\ell+1$\nneighbors other than the other endpoint, increment $\\ell$ and repeat step 2.\nOtherwise stop the skeleton search.\n\n4. Copy the completed skeleton $C$ to a separate graph $P$. For each\nunshielded triple $i-k-j$ in $C$, orient its incident edges in $P$ as\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ exactly when $k\\notin\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$,\nretaining any orientations already made. Keep $C$ unchanged.\n\n5. Repeatedly orient edges of $P$ to closure under the following background-free rules,\nusing fixed lexicographic order on rule instances:\n\n   - **R1:** If $i\\to j-k$ and $i,k$ are nonadjacent, orient $j-k$ as $j\\to k$.\n   - **R2:** If $i-j$ is undirected and $i\\to k\\to j$, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n   - **R3:** If $i-j$, $i-k$, and $i-\\ell$ are undirected, $k\\to j$,\n     $\\ell\\to j$, and $k,\\ell$ are nonadjacent, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n\nDo not apply a background-knowledge rule. Return $C$, the separator record,\nand $P$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2",
            "4.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-dag-markov-equivalence-skeleton-vstructures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Two finite DAGs on the same vertex set are Markov equivalent, meaning that they\ninduce the same d-separation relations, if and only if they have the same\nskeleton and the same unshielded colliders.",
          "uses": [
            "9.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-chordal-components-admit-both-orientations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "An undirected graph is **chordal** when it has no induced cycle of length at\nleast four. A vertex is **simplicial** when all of its neighbors are pairwise\nadjacent. A **perfect elimination order** is a total order in which the later\nneighbors of each vertex form a clique. For every finite chordal undirected\ngraph $H$ and every edge $\\{u,v\\}$ of $H$, there are acyclic orientations of\nall edges of $H$ with no unshielded collider $a\\to w\\leftarrow b$ with\n$a,b$ nonadjacent, one having $u\\to v$ and another having $v\\to u$.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Every consistent extension of a current pattern must respect each R1–R3 conclusion. For R1, the opposite orientation would create the new unshielded collider $i\\to j\\leftarrow k$ with $i,k$ nonadjacent. For R2, the opposite orientation would close the directed path $i\\to k\\to j$ into a directed cycle. For R3, if $j\\to i$, acyclicity forces $k\\to i$ and $\\ell\\to i$ (the alternatives $i\\to k$ or $i\\to\\ell$ each close a directed cycle through $j$); these arrows create the new unshielded collider $k\\to i\\leftarrow\\ell$, since $k,\\ell$ are nonadjacent. Each contradiction is forbidden for a consistent extension. Thus all rule orientations are compelled, and the original DAG $G$ remains a consistent extension at every stage.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "cases",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Every finite DAG has a topological order. In any nonempty induced subgraph, if every vertex had an incoming arrow, repeatedly following incoming arrows would revisit a vertex and create a directed cycle. Thus a source exists; remove one source and repeat on the remaining finite DAG. Listing the removed vertices in order gives a topological order, because an arrow's tail must be removed before its head. This argument uses only finite choices.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "The R1–R3 closure reaches a final pattern after finitely many applications. Each scan checks finitely many rule instances on the finite vertex set; every nonterminal scan directs at least one previously undirected edge, and there are at most $\\binom{|V|}{2}$ such edges. Therefore after finitely many scans a full scan applies no rule, so the final closed pattern $P$ used below exists.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Write $P$ for the final closed pattern. We claim that whenever $a\\to b$ and $b-c$ is undirected in $P$, the edge $a-c$ is directed $a\\to c$. If $a,c$ were nonadjacent, R1 would orient $b-c$ as $b\\to c$, contradicting closure. If $c\\to a$, then $c\\to a\\to b$ and R2 would orient $c-b$ as $c\\to b$, also contradicting closure. It remains to rule out $a-c$ undirected.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.3",
            "cases",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Suppose such an undirected $a-c$ exists, and choose a counterexample for which the arrow $a\\to b$ was first oriented as early as possible in the finite sequence of rule applications. If $a\\to b$ was initially oriented as part of a collider $a\\to b\\leftarrow d$, then $a,d$ are nonadjacent. Since $d\\to b-c$ cannot satisfy R1, $d$ is adjacent to $c$. If $c\\to d$, R2 on $c-b$ applies along $c\\to d\\to b$. If $d\\to c$, R1 on $d\\to c-a$ gives $c\\to a$, and R2 on $c-b$ then applies along $c\\to a\\to b$. If $d-c$ is undirected, R3 on $c-b$ applies with its other two vertices $a,d$. Each case contradicts that $P$ is closed.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "cases",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "If $a\\to b$ was added by R1 from $d\\to a$ with $d,b$ nonadjacent, then either R1 on $d\\to a-c$ directly gives $a\\to c$, or $d$ is adjacent to $c$. If $c\\to d$, R2 on $c-a$ along $c\\to d\\to a$ gives $c\\to a$, and R2 on $c-b$ along $c\\to a\\to b$ then applies. If $d\\to c$, R1 on $d\\to c-b$ gives $c\\to b$. If $d-c$ is undirected, the earlier arrow $d\\to a$ together with $a-c$ is a counterexample of smaller orientation time, contrary to the choice in step 3.1. Thus this rule case is impossible.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "cases",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.2",
          "step": "4.2",
          "claim": "If $a\\to b$ was added by R2 from $a\\to d\\to b$, then $d$ must be adjacent to $c$, since otherwise R1 on $d\\to b-c$ orients $b-c$. If $d\\to c$, R2 on $a-c$ along $a\\to d\\to c$ gives $a\\to c$. If $c\\to d$, R2 on $c-b$ along $c\\to d\\to b$ gives $c\\to b$. If $d-c$ is undirected, the earlier arrow $a\\to d$ and $d-c$ form a counterexample of smaller orientation time. All alternatives contradict closure or minimality.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "cases",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.3",
          "step": "4.3",
          "claim": "If $a\\to b$ was added by R3, at that time there were nonadjacent $d,e$ with earlier arrows $d\\to b$, $e\\to b$ and undirected $a-d$, $a-e$. In the final pattern each of $d,e$ is adjacent to $c$, since otherwise R1 orients $b-c$. Neither $c\\to d$ nor $c\\to e$ is possible, since R2 would orient $c-b$. If $d-c$ or $e-c$ were undirected, its earlier arrow into $b$ together with $b-c$ would give a counterexample of smaller orientation time. Thus $d\\to c$ and $e\\to c$ in $P$. If $a\\to d$ or $a\\to e$ now holds, R2 orients $a-c$. The two arrows $d\\to a$ and $e\\to a$ cannot both hold: since $d,e$ are nonadjacent, $G$ would then contain this unshielded collider, so both arrows would already have been oriented initially, contradicting that $a-d,a-e$ were undirected when R3 applied. If exactly one of these edges points into $a$, the other must therefore be undirected (the outgoing case was already excluded), and R1 directs it out of $a$, again forcing $a\\to c$ by R2. Finally, if both remain undirected, R3 with $d\\to c\\leftarrow e$ directs $a-c$. Every possible final orientation contradicts closure or minimality, completing the proof of the claim in step 2.1.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "cases",
            "contradiction",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-5.1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The claim proved in steps 2.1–4.3 excludes a directed edge whose endpoints lie in one undirected component. Indeed, for $a\\to b$ and an undirected simple path $b=v_0-v_1-\\cdots-v_k=a$, necessarily $k\\ge2$. Propagation gives $a\\to v_1,\\ldots,a\\to v_{k-1}$; its last arrow contradicts the undirected edge $v_{k-1}-a$. If an undirected component had an induced cycle of length at least four, take its latest vertex in a topological order of $G$ from step 1.2. Both incident cycle edges point into that vertex in $G$, and their other endpoints are nonadjacent: an undirected chord is excluded by the induced-cycle assumption, and a directed chord is excluded by the preceding component argument. This would be an unshielded collider in $G$ whose edges were not initially oriented, a contradiction. Hence every undirected component is chordal.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 4.3",
            "contradiction",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-6.1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "Contract each undirected component to one vertex and retain the directed edges between components. There are no loops by step 5.1. For a supposed simple cycle of components $C_1\\to C_2\\to\\cdots\\to C_r\\to C_1$, with $r\\ge2$, choose an arrow $x_t\\to y_{t+1}$ for each quotient edge, with cyclic indices. In component $C_t$, the vertices $y_t$ and $x_t$ are joined by an undirected path. The claim proved in step 4.3 propagates the incoming arrow $x_{t-1}\\to y_t$ along that path to $x_{t-1}\\to x_t$. These arrows give $x_1\\to x_2\\to\\cdots\\to x_r\\to x_1$, contradicting that $G$ consistently extends $P$. Therefore the quotient is a finite DAG.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.3",
            "step 5.1",
            "contradiction",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-7.1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "Fix any undirected edge $u-v$ and choose in its component one of the two orientations supplied by [F4] with the desired direction; orient every other nontrivial undirected component by any such acyclic collider-free orientation. Keep all directed edges of $P$. The combined graph is acyclic: a directed cycle contained in one component is excluded by its chosen orientation, while a cycle passing between components would project to a directed cycle in the quotient of step 6.1.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-8.1",
          "step": "8.1",
          "claim": "The combined DAG creates no unshielded collider absent from $P$. Two internal parents of a vertex cannot form one by the collider-free component orientation. If $x\\to v$ is an external directed parent and $w\\to v$ is an internal parent, then $v-w$ was undirected in $P$; the claim proved in step 4.3 forces $x\\to w$ in $P$, so $x,w$ are adjacent and the collider is shielded. Two external directed parents already give a collider present in $P$ whenever they are nonadjacent. Thus the construction is a consistent extension, and the chosen edge $u-v$ has the requested orientation.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 4.3",
            "step 7.1",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-9.1",
          "step": "9.1",
          "claim": "By step 1.1 every consistent extension preserves every arrow of $P$, and by steps 7.1–8.1 each undirected edge occurs in consistent extensions with either orientation. The initial pattern has exactly the unshielded colliders of $G$, and $G$ remains its consistent extension, so every directed unshielded collider in $P$ is also one in $G$; thus $P$ and $G$ have the same skeleton and unshielded colliders. By [F3], every consistent extension is Markov equivalent to $G$. Conversely, any DAG Markov equivalent to $G$ has the same skeleton and colliders by [F3], hence extends the initial pattern; applying the soundness argument in step 1.1 successively shows that it preserves every arrow added by R1–R3 and therefore extends $P$. Thus the consistent extensions of $P$ are exactly $[G]$, and $P$ is its CPDAG.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 7.1",
            "step 8.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
