    "thm-pc-oracle-correctness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-causal-discovery-oracle-regime",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite set of observed vertices, let $G$ be a directed acyclic graph\non $V$, and let $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$ be the endogenous variables of a finite\nrecursive structural causal model whose observed set is all of $V$ and whose\nlatent set is empty. The model is Markovian: its exogenous random elements are\nmutually independent. Require that the full-joint law $P$ of $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$\nhas a topological probability-kernel factorization: for a topological order\n$v_1,\\ldots,v_p$, its joint law is formed by composing probability kernels\n$K_{v_r}(x_{\\operatorname{pa}(v_r)},dx_{v_r})$ that depend only on the parents\nof $v_r$. Also require that $P$ is faithful to $G$, meaning its conditional\nindependences among vertex subvectors are exactly the d-separations in $G$.\nAssume AC for these probability interfaces.\n\nA **perfect conditional-independence oracle** for this regime accepts any\npairwise disjoint $A,B,S\\subseteq V$ and returns “independent” exactly when\n$\\sigma(X_A)$ and $\\sigma(X_B)$ are conditionally independent given\n$\\sigma(X_S)$. The **causally sufficient perfect-oracle regime** consists of\nthe finite observed DAG, its Markovian recursive model and full-joint law, the\nfaithfulness condition, and this exact oracle. No latent vertex is omitted\nfrom the graph supplied to the oracle.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-dag-factorization-implies-d-separation-conditional-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$ be random elements in measurable spaces\nwhose joint law is generated, in a topological order of the finite DAG $G$,\nby probability kernels $K_v(x_{\\operatorname{pa}(v)},dx_v)$. For pairwise\ndisjoint $A,B,C\\subseteq V$, if $C$ d-separates $A$ and $B$ in $G$, then\n$$\\sigma(X_A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(X_B)\\mid\\sigma(X_C).$$\nThis is the global Markov implication. Its converse is the separate\nfaithfulness condition in [[def-faithfulness-of-a-dag-model]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-faithfulness-of-a-dag-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC, and let a joint law $P$ on the measurable vertex variables of a\nfinite DAG $G$ be given. The law is **faithful to $G$** if, for every triple\nof pairwise disjoint vertex sets $A,B,C$, the conditional independence\n$$\\sigma(X_A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(X_B)\\mid\\sigma(X_C)$$\nimplies that $C$ d-separates $A$ and $B$ in $G$. This is the converse of the\nglobal Markov implication; faithfulness is a separate assumption on the\nspecified law, not a consequence of DAG factorization. No claim about Markov\nequivalence, skeletons, or unshielded colliders is included. Here $X_A$ is\nthe finite random vector $(X_v)_{v\\in A}$ with the finite-product\nsigma-algebra; for $A=\\varnothing$ it is the unique random element of the\nempty-product singleton.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-pc-skeleton-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG on $V$. For distinct vertices $i,j\\in V$, the pair\n$\\{i,j\\}$ is an edge of the skeleton of $G$ if and only if there is no\n$S\\subseteq V\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$ that d-separates $i$ and $j$ in $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-pc-separating-set-collider",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $i-k-j$ be an unshielded triple in a finite DAG and let\n$S\\subseteq V\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$ d-separate $i$ and $j$. Then the triple is a\nv-structure $i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ if and only if $k\\notin S$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "alg-pc-causal-discovery",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** A finite vertex set $V$, a perfect conditional-independence oracle\nfor a law in the causally sufficient discovery regime, and a fixed total order\nof $V$. For every adjacent pair and conditioning set $S$ tested below, the\noracle returns whether $X_i$ and $X_j$ are conditionally independent given\n$X_S$.\n\n**Output:** An undirected skeleton $C$, a separator record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$\nfor each deleted edge, and a partially directed graph $P$.\n\n1. Initialize $C$ to the complete undirected graph on $V$ and set the level\n$\\ell=0$.\n\n2. In the fixed lexicographic order of unordered vertex pairs, process each\npair $i-j$ that is still an edge of $C$. First test every $\\ell$-element\nsubset of $\\operatorname{Adj}_C(i)\\setminus\\{j\\}$ in lexicographic order;\nthen test every $\\ell$-element subset of\n$\\operatorname{Adj}_C(j)\\setminus\\{i\\}$ in lexicographic order. On the first\noracle result of conditional independence given a tested set $S$, delete\n$i-j$, record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)=\\operatorname{Sep}(j,i)=S$, and do\nnot test that pair again. If no set separates the pair, leave it adjacent.\nAdjacency sets are read from the current graph, including deletions already\nmade at level $\\ell$.\n\n3. If at least one remaining edge has an endpoint with at least $\\ell+1$\nneighbors other than the other endpoint, increment $\\ell$ and repeat step 2.\nOtherwise stop the skeleton search.\n\n4. Copy the completed skeleton $C$ to a separate graph $P$. For each\nunshielded triple $i-k-j$ in $C$, orient its incident edges in $P$ as\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ exactly when $k\\notin\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$,\nretaining any orientations already made. Keep $C$ unchanged.\n\n5. Repeatedly orient edges of $P$ to closure under the following background-free rules,\nusing fixed lexicographic order on rule instances:\n\n   - **R1:** If $i\\to j-k$ and $i,k$ are nonadjacent, orient $j-k$ as $j\\to k$.\n   - **R2:** If $i-j$ is undirected and $i\\to k\\to j$, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n   - **R3:** If $i-j$, $i-k$, and $i-\\ell$ are undirected, $k\\to j$,\n     $\\ell\\to j$, and $k,\\ell$ are nonadjacent, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n\nDo not apply a background-knowledge rule. Return $C$, the separator record,\nand $P$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-meek-rules-complete-for-cpdag",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG. Start with its skeleton, orient exactly the edges in\nits unshielded colliders, and leave all other edges undirected. Repeatedly apply\nthe background-free rules R1–R3 from the PC algorithm until no rule applies.\nThe resulting partially directed graph is the CPDAG for the Markov-equivalence\nclass of $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-d-separation-in-a-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite directed acyclic graph** (DAG) is a finite vertex set $V$ with an\narrow set $E\\subseteq V\\times V$ and no directed cycle. A **path** from $u$ to\n$v$ is a finite sequence of distinct vertices beginning at $u$ and ending at\n$v$ in which each consecutive pair is joined by an arrow in either direction;\nthe arrows are not required to point along the sequence. An internal vertex of\na path is a **collider on that path** when the two arrows on the path both point\ninto it, and is a **noncollider** otherwise. A vertex $w$ is a **descendant** of\n$v$ if there is a directed path from $v$ to $w$; for this definition\n$\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ includes $v$ itself as well as its descendants.\n\nFor pairwise disjoint vertex sets $X,Y,C\\subseteq V$, a path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is **active given $C$** when every internal noncollider is outside $C$ and\nevery internal collider has at least one member of $\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ in\n$C$. The set $C$ **d-separates** $X$ and $Y$ in $G$ when no path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is active given $C$; write $X\\perp_d Y\\mid C$ in $G$. The empty set of\nvertices is allowed for any of $X,Y,C$; in particular d-separation is vacuous\nif either endpoint set is empty.\n\nFor $T\\subseteq V$, $G_{\\underline T}$ is obtained by deleting every arrow\nwhose tail lies in $T$, and $G_{\\overline T}$ by deleting every arrow whose\nhead lies in $T$. These are graph operations only; they do not by themselves\nspecify a probability law under intervention.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "By [F2], every d-separation in $G$ is a conditional independence in its law. By [F3], every conditional independence is a d-separation. Since the oracle is exact by [F1], it answers “independent” exactly for d-separated pairs and sets.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "The skeleton search terminates. If $|V|\\le1$, it has no pairs to test and stops at level $0$. Otherwise, at each level it processes finitely many pairs and finitely many subsets. An endpoint has at most $|V|-2$ neighbors other than the other endpoint, so at level $\\ell=|V|-2$ no remaining edge can meet the condition requiring at least $\\ell+1$ such neighbors; the level therefore cannot be incremented past this finite bound. The orientation phase scans finitely many rule instances on the finite vertex set. Every successful application directs one previously undirected edge, which is never undirected again, so there are at most $\\binom{|V|}{2}$ successful applications before a scan finds none. Both phases, and hence the algorithm, terminate.",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "For a nonadjacent pair, acyclicity permits labeling its endpoints $i,j$ so that $j$ is not a descendant of $i$. Put $S=\\operatorname{pa}_G(i)$, which excludes both endpoints. Every path starting with an arrow into $i$ is blocked by its first internal vertex, a parent in $S$ and a noncollider. On any path starting with an arrow out of $i$, there must be a first reversal of arrow direction, since $j$ is not a descendant of $i$. The vertex at that reversal is a collider reached by a directed path from $i$. It cannot have a descendant in $S$, since a directed path from that collider to a parent of $i$ would close a directed cycle through $i$. This path is also blocked by [F8]. Thus $S$ d-separates the endpoints.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F8",
            "construct",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Every true skeleton edge remains in the search graph: by [F4] no allowed conditioning set d-separates its endpoints, so step 1.1 makes every oracle answer dependent. For a nonedge, step 1.3 supplies $S=\\operatorname{pa}_G(i)$ after possibly interchanging endpoints. Every vertex of $S$ remains adjacent to $i$, since all true edges remain. At each level $\\ell<|S|$ while this nonedge remains, endpoint $i$ has at least $|S|\\ge\\ell+1$ other neighbors. The stopping condition therefore cannot stop before level $|S|$. If the nonedge has not already been deleted, at that level the search either deletes it on an earlier separating set or tests $S$ and deletes it. Thus the returned skeleton is exactly the skeleton of $G$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.3",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Every set recorded when an edge is deleted was accepted by the exact oracle. By step 1.1 it d-separates the deleted endpoints. Since the skeleton from step 2.1 is exact, each unshielded triple is an unshielded triple of $G$; [F5] shows the algorithm orients its middle vertex as a collider exactly when it is a collider in $G$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The algorithm then applies R1–R3 to closure. Its pattern has the exact skeleton and exactly the unshielded colliders of $G$ by steps 2.1–3.1. Applying [F7] gives the CPDAG of $G$. By step 1.2 the finite procedure terminates, so this is the algorithm's output.",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
