    "thm-pc-high-dimensional-consistency": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-sample-partial-correlation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $x^{(1)},\\ldots,x^{(n)}\\in\\mathbb R^p$ be a sample, let $i\\ne j$, and\nlet $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,p\\}\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$. Let $\\mathbf x_k\\in\\mathbb\nR^n$ be the sample column for coordinate $k$, and let $\\Pi_S$ be the orthogonal\nprojection onto the span of the all-ones vector and $\\{\\mathbf x_s:s\\in S\\}$.\nWhen the sample covariance matrix of coordinates $\\{i,j\\}\\cup S$ is positive\ndefinite, define the residual columns\n$$R_{i\\mid S}=(I-\\Pi_S)\\mathbf x_i,\\qquad R_{j\\mid S}=(I-\\Pi_S)\\mathbf x_j.$$\nThe **sample partial correlation** is\n$$\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S}=\\frac{R_{i\\mid S}^{T}R_{j\\mid S}}{\\|R_{i\\mid S}\\|_2\\,\\|R_{j\\mid S}\\|_2}.$$\nFor $-1<r<1$, the **Fisher transform** is\n$g(r)=\\frac12\\log\\frac{1+r}{1-r}$, and the sample Fisher statistic is\n$Z_{ij\\mid S}=g(\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S})$. For a test using multiplier\n$\\sqrt{n-|S|-3}$, require $n-|S|-3>0$ so that this factor is positive. Under independent samples\nfrom a nondegenerate Gaussian law, the stated covariance block is positive\ndefinite almost surely whenever $n-|S|-3>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-gaussian-partial-correlation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ have a nondegenerate multivariate-normal\nlaw with mean $\\mu$ and positive-definite covariance matrix $\\Sigma$. For\ndistinct indices $i,j$ and $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,p\\}\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$, define\nthe conditional covariance block\n$$\nK_{ij\\mid S}=\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},\\{i,j\\}}-\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},S}\\Sigma_{S,S}^{-1}\\Sigma_{S,\\{i,j\\}},\n$$\nwith $K_{ij\\mid\\varnothing}=\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},\\{i,j\\}}$. Whenever the diagonal\nentries of this block are positive, the **partial correlation** is\n$$\n\\rho_{ij\\mid S}=\\frac{(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12}}{\\sqrt{(K_{ij\\mid S})_{11}(K_{ij\\mid S})_{22}}}.\n$$\nThe nondegenerate Gaussian case makes the conditioning covariance block\ninvertible; the following theorem proves that $K_{ij\\mid S}$ is positive\ndefinite, so this definition applies to every such $i,j,S$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-zero-partial-correlation-is-conditional-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $X\\sim N_p(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ be a nondegenerate multivariate-normal\nvector, so $\\Sigma$ is positive definite. For distinct $i,j$ and\n$S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,p\\}\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$, the block\n$K_{ij\\mid S}$ in the definition of Gaussian partial correlation is positive\ndefinite, and\n$$ \\rho_{ij\\mid S}=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma(X_i)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(X_j)\\mid\\sigma(X_S). $$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-pc-fisher-transform-uniform-concentration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $0\\le M<1$. There are constants $c_M>0$ and $C_M<\\infty$ such that the\nfollowing holds. Let $X^{(1)},\\ldots,X^{(n)}$ be independent observations from\na nondegenerate Gaussian law, and let $i,j,S$ be as in the definitions of\npopulation and sample partial correlation. Suppose\n$|\\rho_{ij\\mid S}|\\le M$ and $n-|S|-3>0$. Then, for $0<t\\le1$,\n$$ \\mathbb P\\!\\left(\\left|g(\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S})-g(\\rho_{ij\\mid S})\\right|>t\\right) \\le C_M\\exp\\!\\left(-c_M(n-|S|-1)t^2\\right), $$\nwhere $g(r)=\\tfrac12\\log((1+r)/(1-r))$. The constants do not depend on\n$i,j,S,n$ or the Gaussian mean and covariance beyond the bound $M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-pc-oracle-correctness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and the causally sufficient perfect-oracle discovery regime for a\nfinite observed DAG $G$ and its full-joint law. The population PC algorithm\nwith any fixed vertex order terminates and returns exactly the skeleton of $G$\nand records a d-separating set for every deleted pair. At its\ncollider-orientation stage it orients exactly the unshielded colliders of $G$;\nafter R1–R3 closure it returns the CPDAG for the Markov-equivalence class of\n$G$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "alg-pc-causal-discovery",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** A finite vertex set $V$, a perfect conditional-independence oracle\nfor a law in the causally sufficient discovery regime, and a fixed total order\nof $V$. For every adjacent pair and conditioning set $S$ tested below, the\noracle returns whether $X_i$ and $X_j$ are conditionally independent given\n$X_S$.\n\n**Output:** An undirected skeleton $C$, a separator record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$\nfor each deleted edge, and a partially directed graph $P$.\n\n1. Initialize $C$ to the complete undirected graph on $V$ and set the level\n$\\ell=0$.\n\n2. In the fixed lexicographic order of unordered vertex pairs, process each\npair $i-j$ that is still an edge of $C$. First test every $\\ell$-element\nsubset of $\\operatorname{Adj}_C(i)\\setminus\\{j\\}$ in lexicographic order;\nthen test every $\\ell$-element subset of\n$\\operatorname{Adj}_C(j)\\setminus\\{i\\}$ in lexicographic order. On the first\noracle result of conditional independence given a tested set $S$, delete\n$i-j$, record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)=\\operatorname{Sep}(j,i)=S$, and do\nnot test that pair again. If no set separates the pair, leave it adjacent.\nAdjacency sets are read from the current graph, including deletions already\nmade at level $\\ell$.\n\n3. If at least one remaining edge has an endpoint with at least $\\ell+1$\nneighbors other than the other endpoint, increment $\\ell$ and repeat step 2.\nOtherwise stop the skeleton search.\n\n4. Copy the completed skeleton $C$ to a separate graph $P$. For each\nunshielded triple $i-k-j$ in $C$, orient its incident edges in $P$ as\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ exactly when $k\\notin\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$,\nretaining any orientations already made. Keep $C$ unchanged.\n\n5. Repeatedly orient edges of $P$ to closure under the following background-free rules,\nusing fixed lexicographic order on rule instances:\n\n   - **R1:** If $i\\to j-k$ and $i,k$ are nonadjacent, orient $j-k$ as $j\\to k$.\n   - **R2:** If $i-j$ is undirected and $i\\to k\\to j$, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n   - **R3:** If $i-j$, $i-k$, and $i-\\ell$ are undirected, $k\\to j$,\n     $\\ell\\to j$, and $k,\\ell$ are nonadjacent, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n\nDo not apply a background-knowledge rule. Return $C$, the separator record,\nand $P$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-dag-factorization-implies-d-separation-conditional-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$ be random elements in measurable spaces\nwhose joint law is generated, in a topological order of the finite DAG $G$,\nby probability kernels $K_v(x_{\\operatorname{pa}(v)},dx_v)$. For pairwise\ndisjoint $A,B,C\\subseteq V$, if $C$ d-separates $A$ and $B$ in $G$, then\n$$\\sigma(X_A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(X_B)\\mid\\sigma(X_C).$$\nThis is the global Markov implication. Its converse is the separate\nfaithfulness condition in [[def-faithfulness-of-a-dag-model]].",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-faithfulness-of-a-dag-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC, and let a joint law $P$ on the measurable vertex variables of a\nfinite DAG $G$ be given. The law is **faithful to $G$** if, for every triple\nof pairwise disjoint vertex sets $A,B,C$, the conditional independence\n$$\\sigma(X_A)\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\sigma(X_B)\\mid\\sigma(X_C)$$\nimplies that $C$ d-separates $A$ and $B$ in $G$. This is the converse of the\nglobal Markov implication; faithfulness is a separate assumption on the\nspecified law, not a consequence of DAG factorization. No claim about Markov\nequivalence, skeletons, or unshielded colliders is included. Here $X_A$ is\nthe finite random vector $(X_v)_{v\\in A}$ with the finite-product\nsigma-algebra; for $A=\\varnothing$ it is the unique random element of the\nempty-product singleton.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-pc-skeleton-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG on $V$. For distinct vertices $i,j\\in V$, the pair\n$\\{i,j\\}$ is an edge of the skeleton of $G$ if and only if there is no\n$S\\subseteq V\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$ that d-separates $i$ and $j$ in $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-causal-discovery-oracle-regime",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite set of observed vertices, let $G$ be a directed acyclic graph\non $V$, and let $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$ be the endogenous variables of a finite\nrecursive structural causal model whose observed set is all of $V$ and whose\nlatent set is empty. The model is Markovian: its exogenous random elements are\nmutually independent. Require that the full-joint law $P$ of $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$\nhas a topological probability-kernel factorization: for a topological order\n$v_1,\\ldots,v_p$, its joint law is formed by composing probability kernels\n$K_{v_r}(x_{\\operatorname{pa}(v_r)},dx_{v_r})$ that depend only on the parents\nof $v_r$. Also require that $P$ is faithful to $G$, meaning its conditional\nindependences among vertex subvectors are exactly the d-separations in $G$.\nAssume AC for these probability interfaces.\n\nA **perfect conditional-independence oracle** for this regime accepts any\npairwise disjoint $A,B,S\\subseteq V$ and returns “independent” exactly when\n$\\sigma(X_A)$ and $\\sigma(X_B)$ are conditionally independent given\n$\\sigma(X_S)$. The **causally sufficient perfect-oracle regime** consists of\nthe finite observed DAG, its Markovian recursive model and full-joint law, the\nfaithfulness condition, and this exact oracle. No latent vertex is omitted\nfrom the graph supplied to the oracle.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-d-separation-in-a-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite directed acyclic graph** (DAG) is a finite vertex set $V$ with an\narrow set $E\\subseteq V\\times V$ and no directed cycle. A **path** from $u$ to\n$v$ is a finite sequence of distinct vertices beginning at $u$ and ending at\n$v$ in which each consecutive pair is joined by an arrow in either direction;\nthe arrows are not required to point along the sequence. An internal vertex of\na path is a **collider on that path** when the two arrows on the path both point\ninto it, and is a **noncollider** otherwise. A vertex $w$ is a **descendant** of\n$v$ if there is a directed path from $v$ to $w$; for this definition\n$\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ includes $v$ itself as well as its descendants.\n\nFor pairwise disjoint vertex sets $X,Y,C\\subseteq V$, a path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is **active given $C$** when every internal noncollider is outside $C$ and\nevery internal collider has at least one member of $\\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ in\n$C$. The set $C$ **d-separates** $X$ and $Y$ in $G$ when no path joining $X$ to\n$Y$ is active given $C$; write $X\\perp_d Y\\mid C$ in $G$. The empty set of\nvertices is allowed for any of $X,Y,C$; in particular d-separation is vacuous\nif either endpoint set is empty.\n\nFor $T\\subseteq V$, $G_{\\underline T}$ is obtained by deleting every arrow\nwhose tail lies in $T$, and $G_{\\overline T}$ by deleting every arrow whose\nhead lies in $T$. These are graph operations only; they do not by themselves\nspecify a probability law under intervention.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $q_n=O(n^{1-b})$ with $b>0$, eventually $n-q_n-3>0$, so [F1] makes every sample statistic in the capped search defined almost surely. The sample procedure terminates for every answer sequence: it processes at most $q_n+1$ finite levels, each with finitely many pair-and-subset tests, and its finite orientation phase either stops at a conflict or cycle, or directs at most $\\binom{p_n}{2}$ edges. Since $c_n\\le M<1$, take $t=c_n/4\\le1$ in [F4]. For every tested triple $(i,j,S)$, the probability that $|g(\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S})-g(\\rho_{ij\\mid S})|>c_n/4$ is at most $C_M\\exp(-c_M(n-|S|-1)c_n^2/16)$. For large $n$, $q_n/n\\to0$ and $n-q_n-1\\ge n/2$, so each such probability is at most $C_M\\exp(-c'_M n c_n^2)$ for a constant $c'_M>0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For any nonedge of $G_n$, label its endpoints $i,j$ so that $j$ is not a descendant of $i$, as acyclicity permits, and put $S=\\operatorname{pa}_{G_n}(i)$. This set excludes both endpoints and has size at most $q_n$. A path entering $i$ is blocked at its first internal vertex, a conditioned parent and noncollider. A path initially leaving $i$ has a first reversal, since $j$ is not a descendant. At that reversal is a collider reached by a directed path from $i$; it has no descendant in $S$, since otherwise a directed path back through a parent would close a directed cycle through $i$. Thus every such path is blocked by [F10], and $S$ d-separates $i,j$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F10",
            "construct",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "There are at most $N_n=\\binom{p_n}{2}\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{q_n}\\binom{p_n-2}{\\ell} \\le p_n^2(q_n+1)p_n^{q_n}\\le p_n^{q_n+3}$ distinct pair-and-set tests, since $q_n\\le p_n-1$ and $q_n+1\\le p_n$. A union bound, which requires no independence between tests, shows that the event $E_n$ on which every test of size at most $q_n$ has Fisher-transform error at most $c_n/4$ has failure probability at most $C_M\\exp\\!\\left((q_n+3)\\log p_n-c'_M n c_n^2\\right).$ The assumptions imply $n c_n^2\\ge C_0 n^{1-2d}$ eventually and $(q_n+3)\\log p_n=O(n^{1-b}\\log n)=o(n^{1-2d})$, because $2d<b$. Thus, after reducing the positive constant in the exponent, $\\mathbb P(E_n^c)\\le\\exp(-C n^{1-2d})$ for all sufficiently large $n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "On $E_n$, if $\\rho_{ij\\mid S}=0$, then [F1] gives $|g(\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S})|\\le c_n/4$. By [F3], this is the population conditional-independence case. Hence $T_{ij\\mid S}\\le\\sqrt n c_n/4<\\sqrt n c_n/2$, so the test declares independence. If $\\rho_{ij\\mid S}\\ne0$, then [F2] and the lower-bound hypothesis give $|\\rho_{ij\\mid S}|\\ge c_n$, and $|g(\\rho_{ij\\mid S})|\\ge|\\rho_{ij\\mid S}|$ because $|\\operatorname{atanh}(r)|\\ge|r|$ for $|r|<1$. Therefore $|g(\\widehat\\rho_{ij\\mid S})|\\ge3c_n/4$. Since $(n-|S|-3)/n\\ge(n-q_n-3)/n\\to1$, for all sufficiently large $n$ we have $\\sqrt{(n-|S|-3)/n}>2/3$, so $T_{ij\\mid S}>\\sqrt n c_n/2$ and the test declares dependence. By [F3] these are exactly the dependent cases.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "On $E_n$ the capped sample PC search makes the same decisions as the uncapped perfect-oracle procedure through level $q_n$. True edges are never removed, because [F8] excludes d-separation for their endpoints and [F7] makes every such test dependent. For each nonedge, step 1.2 supplies a separating parent set of size at most $q_n$. Its parent edges remain, so while the nonedge remains the endpoint has enough neighbors to prevent stopping before the separator's level. At that level either an earlier set deletes the nonedge or the parent set is tested and deletes it. Thus every nonedge is removed no later than level $q_n$. Thus by completion of that level the sample search and the perfect-oracle run have the exact skeleton and the same separating records. The oracle run cannot need another level: on an exact skeleton of maximum degree at most $q_n$, no remaining edge can have an endpoint with the $q_n+1$ other neighbors required to advance after level $q_n$. If either run stops earlier by its ordinary degree condition, their identical decisions make both stop; otherwise both stop after level $q_n$, the sample run by its cap. Their collider and R1–R3 orientations therefore agree. The oracle orientations are consistent with $G_n$ by [F5], so neither a conflict nor a directed cycle occurs on $E_n$. Hence the failure branch is never used on $E_n$, and [F5] identifies the oracle run's output, hence the sample output, as the CPDAG of $G_n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-5.1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The only failure event is contained in $E_n^c$, whose probability has the bound from step 2.1. Step 4.1 identifies the output on $E_n$ as the CPDAG. This proves the stated consistency bound. The threshold uses $c_n$, so the theorem does not provide a data-driven choice of that parameter.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
