    "ex-pc-latent-confounding-limitations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-recursive-structural-causal-model-and-dag",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite DAG $G=(V,E)$ and a specified topological ordering\n$v_1,\\ldots,v_n$ (every arrow points from an earlier to a later vertex).\nPartition $V=O\\sqcup H$ into **observed** vertices $O$ and **latent** vertices\n$H$. For each $v\\in V$, specify a measurable state space $(S_v,\\mathcal\nS_v)$, a measurable exogenous space $(E_v,\\mathcal E_v)$, a jointly\nmeasurable structural function\n$$f_v:\\prod_{u\\in\\operatorname{pa}(v)}S_u\\times E_v\\to S_v,$$\nand an exogenous random element $U_v$ with values in $E_v$. Here\n$\\operatorname{pa}(v)=\\{u:(u,v)\\in E\\}$; the empty product is a singleton.\nThe model is **Markovian** when the finite family $(U_v)_{v\\in V}$ is mutually\nindependent. No parametric form or distributional truth is asserted. Correlated\ndisturbances may be represented by adding their common causes as latent\nvertices, after which the exogenous family in the expanded model is required\nto be mutually independent.\n\nRecursion in the topological order defines the endogenous random elements\nuniquely by $X_v=f_v(X_{\\operatorname{pa}(v)},U_v)$. The full-joint law is the\nlaw of $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$; the observed law is its marginal law on $O$. An\nobserved-only DAG factorization is asserted only when $H=\\varnothing$ or when\nan additional stated latent-variable condition proves it.\n\nFor $I\\subseteq V$ and a value $a=(a_v)_{v\\in I}\\in\\prod_{v\\in I}S_v$,\n**$do(X_I=a)$** means replace only the equations indexed by $I$ by the\nconstants $X_v=a_v$, leaving every other structural function and every\nexogenous law unchanged. Recursion then defines counterfactual values\n$X_w^a$ for every vertex $w$; the interventional law is their joint law. The\nresponse map $(a,\\omega)\\mapsto X_w^a(\\omega)$ is jointly measurable, by\ninduction through the finite topological order and joint measurability of each\n$f_v$. This permits evaluation at a random treatment value, such as\n$X_w^{M^a}$. When $I=\\{A\\}$, write $X_w^a$ for the response under\n$do(A=a)$. If the factual treatment is $A$, then on $\\{A=a\\}$ recursion\ngives $X_w=X_w^a$ for every vertex $w$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-causal-discovery-oracle-regime",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite set of observed vertices, let $G$ be a directed acyclic graph\non $V$, and let $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$ be the endogenous variables of a finite\nrecursive structural causal model whose observed set is all of $V$ and whose\nlatent set is empty. The model is Markovian: its exogenous random elements are\nmutually independent. Require that the full-joint law $P$ of $(X_v)_{v\\in V}$\nhas a topological probability-kernel factorization: for a topological order\n$v_1,\\ldots,v_p$, its joint law is formed by composing probability kernels\n$K_{v_r}(x_{\\operatorname{pa}(v_r)},dx_{v_r})$ that depend only on the parents\nof $v_r$. Also require that $P$ is faithful to $G$, meaning its conditional\nindependences among vertex subvectors are exactly the d-separations in $G$.\nAssume AC for these probability interfaces.\n\nA **perfect conditional-independence oracle** for this regime accepts any\npairwise disjoint $A,B,S\\subseteq V$ and returns “independent” exactly when\n$\\sigma(X_A)$ and $\\sigma(X_B)$ are conditionally independent given\n$\\sigma(X_S)$. The **causally sufficient perfect-oracle regime** consists of\nthe finite observed DAG, its Markovian recursive model and full-joint law, the\nfaithfulness condition, and this exact oracle. No latent vertex is omitted\nfrom the graph supplied to the oracle.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-independent-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be random elements on a\ncommon probability space. For each $i$, write\n\n$$\\sigma(X_i):=\\{X_i^{-1}(B):B\\in\\Sigma_i\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal F.$$\n\nThe family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is **independent** when the sigma-algebras\n$(\\sigma(X_i))_{i\\in I}$ are independent in the sense of\nIndependent sigma-algebras and independent events.\n\nWhen $\\Sigma_i$ is a Borel sigma-algebra, this agrees with the notation\n$\\sigma(X_i)$ from The sigma-algebra generated by a function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Define $\\gamma(E)=\\int_E e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,dx$ for Borel E in $\\mathbb R$. By The standard normal density has total mass one and The indefinite integral of a nonnegative measurable function is a measure, gamma is a probability measure; denote it $N(0,1)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, define $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ as the law of $x\\mapsto m+\\sigma x$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B,\\gamma)$. This affine map is continuous: for $\\sigma$>0 choose $\\delta$=$\\varepsilon$/$\\sigma$, and for $\\sigma$=0 it is constant. Its inverse images of opens are open, so it is Borel measurable. The law of a random element is a probability measure makes its pushforward a probability. When $\\sigma$=0, the preimage of E is all of R if m belongs to E and empty otherwise, so $N(m,0)=\\delta_m$ in The Dirac set function at a point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-characteristic-functions-under-affine-maps-and-independent-sums",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $a,b,t$, $\\varphi_{aX+b}(t)=e^{itb}\\varphi_X(at)$. For a finite mutually independent family $(X_j)_{j=1}^n$ of real random variables, $\\varphi_{\\sum_jX_j}(t)=\\prod_j\\varphi_{X_j}(t)$. The empty sum has characteristic function one.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-characteristic-function-of-a-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. If $X$ has law $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma\\ge0$, then $\\varphi_X(t)=\\exp(imt-\\sigma^2t^2/2)$ for every real $t$. Moreover $\\mathbb EX=m$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sigma^2$, including $\\sigma=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-uniqueness-of-a-law-from-its-characteristic-function",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Two Borel probability laws on $\\mathbb R$ with equal characteristic functions are equal. In particular a real random variable has a real-valued characteristic function if and only if its law is symmetric under $x\\mapsto-x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-characteristic-function-of-a-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. If $X\\sim N_d(m,\\Sigma)$, then $$\\Phi_X(t):=\\mathbb E e^{it\\cdot X}=\\exp(it\\cdot m-t^T\\Sigma t/2)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$ This transform uniquely determines the law, including singular Sigma.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dag-markov-equivalence-skeleton-vstructures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Two finite DAGs on the same vertex set are Markov equivalent, meaning that they\ninduce the same d-separation relations, if and only if they have the same\nskeleton and the same unshielded colliders.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-cpdag-markov-equivalence-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite vertex set $V$. For a DAG $G$ on $V$, its **skeleton** is the\nundirected graph with edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $G$ contains $i\\to j$ or\n$j\\to i$. An **unshielded triple** is a triple of distinct vertices $i,k,j$\nwith edges $i-k$ and $k-j$ in the skeleton and no edge between $i$ and $j$.\nIt is an **unshielded collider** (also called a v-structure) when its arrows are\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$. Two DAGs on $V$ are **Markov equivalent** when they\ninduce the same d-separation relations: for every pairwise disjoint\n$A,B,S\\subseteq V$, $A$ and $B$ are d-separated by $S$ in one DAG exactly\nwhen they are d-separated by $S$ in the other. The equivalence class of $G$ is\ndenoted $[G]$.\n\nA **partially directed acyclic graph** (PDAG) on $V$ is a simple mixed graph\nwhose directed edges contain no directed cycle. A **consistent DAG extension**\nof a PDAG preserves every directed edge, orients every undirected edge, is\nacyclic, and creates no unshielded collider absent from the PDAG. For an\nequivalence class with a common skeleton, its **completed pattern** directs an\nedge exactly when that orientation occurs in every DAG of the class and leaves\nthe edge undirected when both orientations occur. A **completed partially\ndirected acyclic graph** (CPDAG) for $[G]$ is this unique completed pattern\nwhen its consistent DAG extensions are exactly $[G]$. Thus its directed edges\nare compelled, while each undirected edge occurs in both directions among its\nconsistent extensions.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "alg-pc-causal-discovery",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** A finite vertex set $V$, a perfect conditional-independence oracle\nfor a law in the causally sufficient discovery regime, and a fixed total order\nof $V$. For every adjacent pair and conditioning set $S$ tested below, the\noracle returns whether $X_i$ and $X_j$ are conditionally independent given\n$X_S$.\n\n**Output:** An undirected skeleton $C$, a separator record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$\nfor each deleted edge, and a partially directed graph $P$.\n\n1. Initialize $C$ to the complete undirected graph on $V$ and set the level\n$\\ell=0$.\n\n2. In the fixed lexicographic order of unordered vertex pairs, process each\npair $i-j$ that is still an edge of $C$. First test every $\\ell$-element\nsubset of $\\operatorname{Adj}_C(i)\\setminus\\{j\\}$ in lexicographic order;\nthen test every $\\ell$-element subset of\n$\\operatorname{Adj}_C(j)\\setminus\\{i\\}$ in lexicographic order. On the first\noracle result of conditional independence given a tested set $S$, delete\n$i-j$, record $\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)=\\operatorname{Sep}(j,i)=S$, and do\nnot test that pair again. If no set separates the pair, leave it adjacent.\nAdjacency sets are read from the current graph, including deletions already\nmade at level $\\ell$.\n\n3. If at least one remaining edge has an endpoint with at least $\\ell+1$\nneighbors other than the other endpoint, increment $\\ell$ and repeat step 2.\nOtherwise stop the skeleton search.\n\n4. Copy the completed skeleton $C$ to a separate graph $P$. For each\nunshielded triple $i-k-j$ in $C$, orient its incident edges in $P$ as\n$i\\to k\\leftarrow j$ exactly when $k\\notin\\operatorname{Sep}(i,j)$,\nretaining any orientations already made. Keep $C$ unchanged.\n\n5. Repeatedly orient edges of $P$ to closure under the following background-free rules,\nusing fixed lexicographic order on rule instances:\n\n   - **R1:** If $i\\to j-k$ and $i,k$ are nonadjacent, orient $j-k$ as $j\\to k$.\n   - **R2:** If $i-j$ is undirected and $i\\to k\\to j$, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n   - **R3:** If $i-j$, $i-k$, and $i-\\ell$ are undirected, $k\\to j$,\n     $\\ell\\to j$, and $k,\\ell$ are nonadjacent, orient $i-j$ as $i\\to j$.\n\nDo not apply a background-knowledge rule. Return $C$, the separator record,\nand $P$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-pc-oracle-correctness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and the causally sufficient perfect-oracle discovery regime for a\nfinite observed DAG $G$ and its full-joint law. The population PC algorithm\nwith any fixed vertex order terminates and returns exactly the skeleton of $G$\nand records a d-separating set for every deleted pair. At its\ncollider-orientation stage it orients exactly the unshielded colliders of $G$;\nafter R1–R3 closure it returns the CPDAG for the Markov-equivalence class of\n$G$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Let $W_1,\\ldots,W_m$ be a finite independent family of scalar normal variables with means $\\mu_r$ and variances $\\sigma_r^2$. For real coefficients $a_1,\\ldots,a_m,b$, [F3] gives the characteristic function of $b+\\sum_r a_rW_r$ as $\\exp\\!\\left(it(b+\\sum_r a_r\\mu_r)-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\sum_r a_r^2\\sigma_r^2\\right)$; scalar uniqueness identifies this as the normal law with that mean and variance. Every scalar projection of a finite affine image of $W=(W_1,\\ldots,W_m)$ is such a sum, so [F4] proves that every such image is multivariate normal, including when a projection has variance zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "In the latent model, $\\mathbb E(X,Y)=(0,0)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=2$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1$, by independence of $L,U_X,U_Y$. In the direct-edge model, $\\mathbb E(X,Y)=(0,0)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=2$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1$, and $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\tfrac14(2)+\\tfrac32=2$. Both vectors are Gaussian by step 1.1 and have the same positive-definite covariance matrix $\\Sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, whose leading principal minors are $2$ and $3$. By [F5], both vector characteristic functions equal $\\exp(-t^T\\Sigma t/2)$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R^2$; the uniqueness clause of [F5] gives equality of the full observed laws.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The latent model is a finite recursive Markovian SCM with observed set $\\{X,Y\\}$ and omitted latent vertex $L$, so it does not satisfy [F2]. The direct-edge model has observed set $\\{X,Y\\}$ and independent exogenous variables; its full-joint law factors as $N(0,2)(dx)N(x/2,3/2)(dy)$, the root probability kernel for $X$ followed by the probability kernel for $Y$ given its parent $X$. In its two-vertex graph $X\\to Y$, the only pair of nonempty disjoint endpoint sets is $\\{X\\},\\{Y\\}$, which is not d-separated because of the direct path; step 2.1 gives $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1$, so they are not independent. Thus this law is faithful to the direct-edge DAG, and the direct-edge model satisfies the observed-only Markovian factorization and faithfulness conditions in the discovery regime. Since the observed laws agree by step 2.1, no procedure using only that law can distinguish the two causal explanations.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The common observed law has $\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=1\\ne0$, so $X,Y$ are dependent and the perfect oracle does not delete their only edge. With only two vertices there is no unshielded triple and none of R1–R3 applies; by [F9] the regime-compliant run terminates, and its computed output is $X-Y$. Reversing the single edge in the direct-edge DAG keeps the same skeleton and no unshielded collider, so [F6] proves the reverse DAG is equivalent; [F7] therefore gives the same undirected CPDAG. The PC output reports adjacency but cannot recover the latent-versus-direct causal distinction.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
